WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
Let me show you how Harper Vance works,

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:03.640
from targets during the session

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:05.520
to reviewing what happened afterward.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.680
This is our NQ dashboard.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:10.280
The idea is to give a professional team

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:11.860
a clear record to inspect

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:14.080
rather than just a target and a percentage.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:16.360
I'll walk through today's targets first.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:18.440
Then we'll look at a completed day,

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.020
the weekly summary, and the historical view.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:23.680
Let's start here with the open targets for today.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:24.980
During the session,

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.380
qualifying targets appear in this section.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.980
You can see the target price, the direction,

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:31.580
the recorded timestamps,

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.680
and whether the target is still open.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.500
You can also see the pullback

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.880
or how far price moved away before reaching the target.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:40.540
That gives you some context

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:41.840
while you're looking at the target

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.200
instead of just looking at a price level on its own.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.720
The dashboard checks for updates roughly every 60 seconds,

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:49.880
so this isn't a tick by tick feed.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.180
Below that, the session summary shows

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:53.840
how many targets have been created,

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.860
how many have been reached, and the hit rate so far.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.720
These are the results for a session that's still running.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.180
Now, a strong result on one day

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:03.960
doesn't tell you what will happen tomorrow,

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:05.520
and a target getting hit

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.700
does not mean a profitable trade happened.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.920
Your entry, your stop, the path price took,

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.340
and your execution costs all matter.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:14.740
What we're showing here

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:17.420
is the record of the targets generated by the engine

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.540
with the timing and the statistical analysis around them.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.020
So treat this as information to inspect

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:24.720
alongside your own process,

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:27.380
not a promise about what your next trade will do.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.180
Here's the target log for today.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.340
You can see when each target appeared,

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:34.280
when it was reached, and how long it took to resolve.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.460
You can also see how far price moved away first.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:39.180
That last part matters.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.180
A target getting hit doesn't tell you the whole story.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:43.840
Look at this example here.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.860
It took about an hour and 16 minutes to resolve,

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:50.620
and the recorded pullback was roughly 60.5 points.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:52.500
That's a very different picture

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.060
from simply marking it as a hit.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.260
This is today's record so far.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:57.980
Now let's take those outcomes

00:01:57.980 --> 00:01:59.680
into the daily performance view.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.320
This gives us a different way to review the session.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:04.840
Today's session isn't finished in this recording,

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.100
so I'm opening the previous day's report.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:09.240
Here, you can see the target count

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.340
and the outcomes for that completed day.

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:14.240
In this example, 11 targets were generated

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:15.520
and 10 were reached.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.940
The session replay puts those targets on the NQ chart,

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:20.760
so you can see where they appeared

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:22.400
and which one wasn't reached.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:24.600
You're not just looking at a summary number.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:26.820
You can look at the session behind it.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:28.480
Here's the daily target log.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.240
This example shows a 60-point pullback

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:33.360
on a target generated at 10 o'clock,

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:35.640
which then resolved in about three minutes.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.080
So even a target that resolves quickly

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:40.300
can have a substantial move along the way.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.160
Further down, the third target wasn't reached.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:44.660
It's recorded as missed.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.660
You can inspect that outcome alongside the others,

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:50.180
rather than only seeing the successful targets.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:53.020
That's the point of keeping this detail in the report.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.580
You can compare the timing, the pullback,

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:57.780
and the final outcome for each target.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.040
The report also includes explanations

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:01.600
to help you read these numbers.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.920
Here, the session is broken into morning, midday,

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.080
and afternoon, so you can compare the target outcomes

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:09.580
across different parts of the day.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:11.920
There's missed target analysis as well,

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:15.060
including how close a missed target came to being reached.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:16.580
So that's the daily view.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:20.140
A summary, a replay, individual target records,

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:21.400
and the missed outcomes.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.360
Next, we'll bring several of those sessions together

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:25.960
and look at the weekly summary.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:28.660
The weekly view combines the target outcomes

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:29.740
across a given week.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:32.960
In this recording, week 44 is still in progress.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:36.140
I'm switching to week 43, the previous week,

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:38.260
so we're reviewing a completed period.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.440
At the top, you can see how many targets were generated,

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:44.380
how many were reached, and the hit rate for that week.

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:46.060
There's a weekly log too,

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:48.020
along with a day-by-day breakdown.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:50.520
That lets you move from the overall result

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:53.580
back to the individual days and targets that produced it.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:55.140
This breakdown is useful

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:57.680
because it separates direction and time of day.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:03:59.980
You can look at bullish targets in the morning,

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:02.120
at midday, and in the afternoon,

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:04.500
then compare those with bearish targets

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.040
over the same parts of the session.

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:08.080
There's a 20 session view here

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:09.620
and a separate view for the week.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:11.680
Those aren't the same sample size.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.120
One week gives you a narrower snapshot.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:16.680
20 sessions give you a broader comparison.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:19.860
Check the number of observations behind each result

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:21.200
before drawing a conclusion.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.480
You can also review the missed target resolution information

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:26.880
so the weekly analysis doesn't stop

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:28.240
at the targets that were reached.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:29.800
For a professional team,

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.620
this is another source of information

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.540
to evaluate against the process you're already using.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:36.480
The useful question isn't just

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:38.320
whether the headline number looks good.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.840
It's whether the timing, the pullbacks,

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:42.960
and the missed outcomes give you information

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.520
you can actually use.

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:46.580
Now let's widen the view again

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:48.380
and look at the historical section.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:50.900
Here, the daily and weekly results

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:52.480
are brought together over the month.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:55.240
October is still in progress in this recording,

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:58.440
so I'm opening September to review a completed period.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:00.740
You can see how many sessions are included,

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:02.560
how many targets were generated,

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:04.180
and the recorded outcomes.

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:07.560
This gives you a longer view than a single day or week

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:10.440
while keeping the period and the amount of data in view.

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:11.960
At the end of the day,

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:15.240
we also provide an intraday target resolution report.

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:18.240
This is a written review of how the targets performed

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:19.400
during that session.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:21.460
It covers the same kind of information

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:23.140
you've just seen in the daily view

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:25.240
with more explanation around the results

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:26.380
and how to read them.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:29.140
That gives your team something concrete to inspect

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:30.980
and discuss rather than relying

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:32.580
on a screenshot of a hit rate.

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:35.500
That's the walkthrough of the dashboard and the reporting.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:39.940
If your team works in NQ, visit harpervanceqa.com

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:41.520
and request a complete report.

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:45.040
You can review the actual reporting before discussing access

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:47.060
and decide whether it fits your process.

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:49.500
Thanks for taking the time to look through it with me.

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:50.260
Take care.
